{"study_accession":"PRJEB112179","secondary_study_accession":"ERP192763","title":"Sex-specific immune signatures in irritable bowel syndrome suggest reduced neutrophil-associated activity in males and altered interferon signaling in females","description":"Background & Aims: Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder with a higher prevalence in females. While most transcriptomic studies have focused on gut mucosal biopsies, systemic transcriptional differences remain poorly understood. We investigated sex-specific whole-blood transcriptome profiles in IBS using a cross-sectional observational design.\n\nMethods: Whole-blood bulk RNA sequencing was performed on the DNBSEQ-G400 platform. Differential expression analysis was performed separately for males and females using DESeq2 (FDR < 0.05). Functional enrichment and protein–protein interaction analyses were performed using STRING database. Cell-type enrichment scores were estimated with xCell2. Transcription factor (TF) coordination network analysis was conducted using NetREm. In silico validation was performed using three independent publicly available RNA-seq datasets from small intestine, colon, and peripheral blood mononuclear cells.\n\nResults: We identified sex-specific transcriptional signatures. In males, genes involved in neutrophil-mediated antibacterial responses were downregulated. In females, genes involved in antiviral defense and interferon signaling pathways were downregulated, whereas neutrophil-associated pathways showed opposite regulation. Transcription factor coordination analysis revealed widespread alterations in regulatory relationships, including both shared and sex-specific TF–TF interactions. Cross-cohort comparisons revealed partial concordance across datasets, with both sexes overlapping primarily with small intestine data; males showed gene- and pathway-level overlap, whereas females showed gene-level overlap.\n\nConclusions: Whole-blood transcriptome profiling revealed distinct immune-related signatures in IBS, suggesting sex-specific immune modulation in IBS pathophysiology.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":35,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMEA122301254","study_accession":"PRJEB112179","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Ctrl_M_01","description":"Control Male whole blood 01","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-05-20","attributes":[["phenotype","Control"],["ENA-CHECKLIST","ERC000011:1.3"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["collection date","2021-02-28"],["sex","male"],["scientific_name","Homo sapiens"],["common name","human"],["ibs_subtype","not applicable"],["library_prep_batch","Second"],["lat_lon","56.9677 N 24.1056 E"],["tissue_type","whole blood"],["geographic location (country and/or sea)","Latvia"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"ERR17156146","experiment_accession":"ERX16539672","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"Inverse rRNA","instrument_model":"DNBSEQ-G400","read_count":41808892,"base_count":6271333800,"first_public":"2026-05-20","experiment_title":"DNBSEQ-G400 sequencing"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMEA122301255","study_accession":"PRJEB112179","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"IBS_M_01","description":"IBS Male whole blood 01","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-05-20","attributes":[["phenotype","IBS"],["ENA-CHECKLIST","ERC000011:1.3"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["collection date","2022-01-20"],["sex","male"],["scientific_name","Homo sapiens"],["common name","human"],["ibs_subtype","not provided"],["library_prep_batch","Second"],["lat_lon","56.9677 N 24.1056 E"],["tissue_type","whole blood"],["geographic location (country and/or sea)","Latvia"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"ERR17156168","experiment_accession":"ERX16539694","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"Inverse rRNA","instrument_model":"DNBSEQ-G400","r